var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"schemes/piecewise_linear/#MM2015PiecewiseLinear","page":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear","text":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear() uses the dynamics, active phases, and events of MM2015FixedT and advances each segment with rates frozen at the segment start. It is a forward-Euler step of C1 from each event to the next.","title":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/piecewise_linear/#Segments","page":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear","text":"At the start t_s of a segment, with supersaturations delta_s over liquid and delta_is over ice, the rates are\n\nS_l = lfracdelta_stau_lGamma_l qquad\nS_i = ifracdelta_istau_iGamma_i\n\nand they stay constant over the segment. The vapor budget with constant rates gives both supersaturations the same constant slope,\n\ndelta(t) = delta_s + f(delta_s)(t - t_s) qquad\ndelta_i(t) = delta_is + f(delta_s)(t - t_s) qquad\nq_l(t) = q_l(t_s) + S_l(t - t_s) qquad\nq_i(t) = q_i(t_s) + S_i(t - t_s)\n\nwith f from MM2015FixedT. Because f is linear with slope -1tau, delta reaches the equilibrium delta_mathrmeq = A_ctau at t_s + tau.","title":"Segments"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/piecewise_linear/#Events","page":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear","text":"The events of MM2015FixedT, with linear times, and one more:\n\nEvent Condition Time from the segment start\nliquid saturation delta = 0 -delta_s  f(delta_s) when positive\nice saturation delta_i = 0 -delta_is  f(delta_s) when positive\nliquid exhausted q_l = 0 -q_l(t_s)S_l when positive\nice exhausted q_i = 0 -q_i(t_s)S_i when positive\nequilibrium delta = delta_mathrmeq tau\n\nAfter the equilibrium event, delta stays at delta_mathrmeq and the rates take their equilibrium values until another event or the end of the step. delta_mathrmeq = 0 is an ordinary state.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/piecewise_linear/#Result","page":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear","text":"The mean rates are the summed condensate increments of all segments divided by Delta t.","title":"Result"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/piecewise_linear/#Properties","page":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear","text":"The rates converge to those of MM2015FixedT at first order as Delta t to 0.\nIn a step with one active phase and no other event, the linear segment to the equilibrium and the exponential approach of C5 cover the same area of delta - delta_mathrmeq, (delta_s - delta_mathrmeq)tau. The increments of the two schemes then differ by (delta_s - delta_mathrmeq)taue^-Delta ttau divided by tau_cGamma_c, which vanishes for Delta t gg tau.\nA phase that forms at its saturation boundary starts its segment at delta_s = 0 or delta_is = 0, so its rate stays zero until the equilibrium event, a time tau later.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"Functions and types that the public interface calls, grouped by component. They are not exported and can change between versions.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Coefficients","page":"Internals","text":"The conversion from an MM2015Problem in either moisture basis to the Coefficients of Appendix C.","title":"Coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Frozen-coefficient-equations","page":"Internals","text":"","title":"Frozen-coefficient equations"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Events-and-recorders","page":"Internals","text":"","title":"Events and recorders"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Frozen-coefficient-schemes","page":"Internals","text":"","title":"Frozen-coefficient schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Parcel-model-of-MM2015","page":"Internals","text":"","title":"Parcel model of MM2015"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Event-location-in-MM2015","page":"Internals","text":"","title":"Event location in MM2015"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Exponential-Rosenbrock-step","page":"Internals","text":"","title":"Exponential Rosenbrock step"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.dry_fraction","page":"Internals","text":"Dry-air mass fraction m_d / m from the total water x_t of the basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.dry_fraction"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.specific_humidity","page":"Internals","text":"Specific humidity of a water amount x given in the basis, with dry fraction q_d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.specific_humidity"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_heat_capacity","page":"Internals","text":"Heat capacity per unit basis mass from the moist-air value c_pm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_heat_capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_heat_capacity_derivative","page":"Internals","text":"Derivative of the basis heat capacity with respect to the basis total water, from that of c_pm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_heat_capacity_derivative"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_density","page":"Internals","text":"Density of the basis mass from the moist-air density ρ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_density"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_saturation","page":"Internals","text":"basis_saturation(basis, s::PhaseSaturation, q_d) -> (; x_s, dx_s_dT, dx_s_dp, dx_s_dxt)\n\nSaturation humidity in the basis and its derivatives with respect to temperature, pressure, and total water.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_saturation"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_saturation_second","page":"Internals","text":"basis_saturation_second(basis, s::PhaseSaturation, q_d) -> (; x_s_TT, x_s_Tp, x_s_Txt)\n\nSecond derivatives of the saturation humidity in the basis: ∂²/∂T², ∂²/∂T∂p, and ∂²/∂T∂x_t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.basis_saturation_second"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation_total_water_derivative","page":"Internals","text":"Derivative of the saturation humidity x_s in the basis with respect to the basis total water.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation_total_water_derivative"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.total_water_derivatives","page":"Internals","text":"total_water_derivatives(basis, q_d, q_l, q_i) -> (; dq_t, dq_l, dq_i)\n\nDerivatives of the specific humidities with respect to the total water of the basis, at fixed liquid and ice of the basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.total_water_derivatives"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.relaxation","page":"Internals","text":"relaxation(k, a) -> (; inv_τ, τ, A_δ, A_δi)\n\nInverse relaxation time 1/τ (C2), τ (Inf with neither phase active), and the forcings of dδ/dt = A_δ − δ/τ (C1, C4 with Γ_i) and dδ_i/dt = A_δi − δ_i/τ for the active phases a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.relaxation"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.supersaturation_tendency","page":"Internals","text":"Time derivative of δ and of δ_i (C1) at the supersaturations δ, δ_i with the active phases a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.supersaturation_tendency"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.equilibrium_supersaturations","page":"Internals","text":"Equilibrium supersaturations (; δ = A_δ * τ, δ_i = A_δi * τ) of the active phases a, which must include one phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.equilibrium_supersaturations"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.frozen_evolution","page":"Internals","text":"frozen_evolution(δ_0, δ_i0, inv_τ, τ, A_δ, A_δi, t) -> (; δ, δ_i, I, I_i)\n\nSupersaturations after time t (C5) and their integrals I and I_i over [0, t].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.frozen_evolution"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.condensate_increments","page":"Internals","text":"Liquid and ice increments (; Δx_l, Δx_i) (C6, C7) from the integrals I of δ and I_i of δ_i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.condensate_increments"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation_time","page":"Internals","text":"saturation_time(x_0, inv_τ, τ, A) -> t\n\nFirst time at which a supersaturation following dx/dt = A − x/τ from x_0 reaches zero (the inverse of C5), or Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation_time"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.active_phases","page":"Internals","text":"active_phases(k, δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i) -> ActivePhases\n\nLiquid is active when x_l > 0 or δ > 0. Below the triple point, ice is active when x_i > 0 or δ_i > 0; above it, when x_i > 0 and δ_i < 0. On its saturation boundary, a phase with zero mass is active when its supersaturation rises with that phase inactive, and ice with mass above the triple point is active when δ_i falls.\n\n\n\n\n\nactive_phases(problem, s, below, thresholds = Thresholds(0, 0, 0)) -> ActivePhases\n\nThe rule of active_phases at the parcel_state s, with the time derivatives of the parcel model on the saturation boundaries. A phase without mass forms only when its supersaturation exceeds thresholds.δ_min (liquid) or thresholds.δ_i_min (ice), or lies within it, is not negative, and rises.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.active_phases"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.max_events","page":"Internals","text":"Largest number of events in one step of a frozen-coefficient scheme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.max_events"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.stays_subsaturated","page":"Internals","text":"Whether a parcel without condensate stays below saturation over liquid, and below the triple point over ice, for the time t; with no phase active, both supersaturations change at the rate A_l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.stays_subsaturated"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.evolve","page":"Internals","text":"evolve(scheme, k, Δt, recorder) -> (; Δx_l, Δx_i)\n\nLiquid and ice increments over [0, Δt] of the frozen-coefficient dynamics of k, advanced by scheme from each event to the next, with the state rounded by the thresholds of scheme at the start of each segment. Each segment is passed to recorder.\n\n\n\n\n\nevolve(scheme::MM2015, problem, Δt, recorder) -> (; Δx_l, Δx_i)\n\nLiquid and ice increments over [0, Δt] of the parcel model, integrated by exprb43 with step-size control to the tolerances of scheme. When event indicators turn positive in an accepted step, the first to cross is located on the step map by scheme.root_finder, and the step is shortened to it. Each step is passed to recorder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.evolve"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.NoRecorder","page":"Internals","text":"Recorder that keeps no segments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.NoRecorder"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.SegmentRecorder","page":"Internals","text":"Recorder that keeps every segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.SegmentRecorder"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.advance","page":"Internals","text":"advance(::MM2015FixedT, k, a, δ, δ_i, t) -> (; δ, δ_i, Δx_l, Δx_i)\n\nSupersaturations after time t and the liquid and ice increments, for the active phases a.\n\n\n\n\n\nadvance(::MM2015PiecewiseLinear, k, a, δ, δ_i, t) -> (; δ, δ_i, Δx_l, Δx_i)\n\nSupersaturations after time t and the liquid and ice increments, with the rates at (δ, δ_i) held fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.advance"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.segment","page":"Internals","text":"segment(::MM2015FixedT, k, a, δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i, remaining) -> (; duration, event, δ, δ_i, Δx_l, Δx_i)\n\nDuration of the segment that starts at (δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i) with the active phases a and ends at its first event or after remaining, the event (EndOfStep for the end of the step), and the state change over it.\n\n\n\n\n\nsegment(::MM2015PiecewiseLinear, k, a, δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i, remaining) -> (; duration, event, δ, δ_i, Δx_l, Δx_i)\n\nDuration of the segment that starts at (δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i) with the active phases a and ends at its first event or after remaining, the event (EndOfStep for the end of the step), and the state change over it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.segment"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.linear_segment","page":"Internals","text":"linear_segment(k, a, δ, δ_i) -> (; S_l, S_i, slope, τ)\n\nLiquid and ice rates at (δ, δ_i), the common slope of δ and δ_i, and the time τ at which the linear path reaches the equilibrium of the active phases a. At that equilibrium the slope is zero and τ = Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.linear_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.linear_time","page":"Internals","text":"Time for x to reach zero at the rate slope, or Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.linear_time"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.parcel_state","page":"Internals","text":"Thermodynamic quantities of the parcel model at time t and state u = (x_l, x_i, T), per unit basis mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.parcel_state"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.parcel_tendencies","page":"Internals","text":"Liquid and ice rates and the temperature tendency (; liq, ice, T) at the parcel state s with active phases a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.parcel_tendencies"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.ParcelRHS","page":"Internals","text":"Right side of the parcel model with fixed active phases, called as rhs(t, u).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.ParcelRHS"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.parcel_linearization","page":"Internals","text":"parcel_linearization(problem, a, s) -> (; F, J, v)\n\nRight side F, Jacobian J = ∂F/∂u (by columns), and v = ∂F/∂t of the parcel model with active phases a at the parcel_state s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.parcel_linearization"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.supersaturation_rates","page":"Internals","text":"Time derivatives (; dδ, dδ_i) of δ and δ_i at the parcel_state s, from parcel_tendencies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.supersaturation_rates"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.below_triple","page":"Internals","text":"Whether the parcel at the parcel_state s counts as below the triple point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.below_triple"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.rounded_supersaturations","page":"Internals","text":"Supersaturations (; δ, δ_i) over liquid and over ice at the parcel_state s, zero below thresholds.δ_min and thresholds.δ_i_min in magnitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.rounded_supersaturations"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.return_small_condensate","page":"Internals","text":"return_small_condensate(problem, t, u, u_lo, x_min) -> (; u, u_lo, r_l, r_i)\n\nThe state u + u_lo = (x_l, x_i, T) with liquid and ice below x_min returned to the vapor, the temperature lowered by their latent heat, and the returned amounts r_l and r_i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.return_small_condensate"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.two_sum","page":"Internals","text":"(s, e) with s = fl(a + b) and a + b = s + e exactly (Knuth's TwoSum).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.two_sum"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.compensated_add","page":"Internals","text":"The state u + u_lo advanced by Δu, as a rounded state and its rounding error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.compensated_add"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.scaled_error","page":"Internals","text":"Largest component of the local error estimate relative to atol + rtol max(|u|, |u_next|).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.scaled_error"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.step_start","page":"Internals","text":"step_start(scheme, problem, t, u, u_lo, scaling) -> NamedTuple\n\nStart of an integrator step from (t, u + u_lo): the state with the condensate below the threshold of scheme returned to the vapor, the amounts returned, the parcel state, the active phases, the event indicators and their rates, and the linearization with its balancing warm-started from scaling. Returns the fields u, u_lo, r_l, r_i, state, below, a, g, ġ, F, J, v, and scaling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.step_start"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.event_indicators","page":"Internals","text":"event_indicators(problem, a, below, s, thresholds = Thresholds(0, 0, 0)) -> NTuple{4}\n\nValues at the parcel_state s that end a segment when one becomes positive: -x_l (active liquid) or δ; -x_i for active ice; δ_i for inactive ice below the triple point and active ice above it, -δ_i for inactive ice with mass above it; and the signed distance past T_triple. -Inf marks an unused indicator. For a phase without mass the supersaturation is that of rounded_supersaturations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.event_indicators"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.event_indicator_rates","page":"Internals","text":"Time derivatives of event_indicators along the parcel model at the parcel_state s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.event_indicator_rates"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.StepIndicators","page":"Internals","text":"Event indicators after a step of length s from (t, u + u_lo) with the linearization (F, J, v), called as indicators(s); indicators(s, k) is the k-th.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.StepIndicators"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.StepIndicator","page":"Internals","text":"Indicator k of indicators::StepIndicators as a function of the step length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.StepIndicator"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.indicator_rounding","page":"Internals","text":"Rounding error of event indicator k of event_indicators with active phases a at the parcel_state s: eps times the size of the quantities it is formed from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.indicator_rounding"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.earliest_fired","page":"Internals","text":"Index of the fired indicator (g₁ > 0) whose linear interpolation from g₀ crosses zero first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.earliest_fired"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.event_kind","page":"Internals","text":"Event kind of indicator k of event_indicators with active phases a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.event_kind"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.masked","page":"Internals","text":"The indicators g with those not selected replaced by -Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.masked"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.all_but","page":"Internals","text":"Selection of every indicator except k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.all_but"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.hermite_maximum","page":"Internals","text":"Largest value on [0, 1] of the cubic Hermite interpolant of (g₀, h ġ₀) at 0 and (g₁, h ġ₁) at 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.hermite_maximum"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.hidden_crossing","page":"Internals","text":"Whether an indicator can cross zero inside a step of length h without a sign change at its ends: the cubic Hermite interpolant of its values g, g_next and rates ġ, ġ_next rises above zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.hidden_crossing"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Vec3","page":"Internals","text":"3-vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Vec3"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Mat3","page":"Internals","text":"3×3 matrix, stored by columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Mat3"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.opnorm1","page":"Internals","text":"Largest absolute column sum of A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.opnorm1"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.add_diagonal","page":"Internals","text":"M with c added to its diagonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.add_diagonal"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.balancing","page":"Internals","text":"balancing(A, start = identity_scaling(FT)) -> (to, from)\n\nEntrywise factors to[i, j] = d_j/d_i and from[i, j] = d_i/d_j of the diagonal similarity B = D⁻¹ A D = A .* to, D = diag(d) with powers of 2 that bring the off-diagonal 1-norms of each row and column of B within a factor 4 of each other (Parlett & Reinsch 1969, Numer. Math. 13, 293–304); f(A) = f(B) .* from for a function f of the matrix. D = I when B does not have the smaller 1-norm. The balance of A also balances every multiple of A. The iteration starts from the factors start, such as those of a nearby matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.balancing"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.similarity_factors","page":"Internals","text":"(to, from) of balancing from the diagonal d and its inverse, or ones unless they lower ‖A‖₁.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.similarity_factors"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.identity_scaling","page":"Internals","text":"All-ones factors: the identity similarity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.identity_scaling"},{"category":"constant","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.PHI_TAYLOR_RADIUS","page":"Internals","text":"Largest ‖Z‖₁ at which the Taylor series of φ₄ is summed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.PHI_TAYLOR_RADIUS"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_taylor_terms","page":"Internals","text":"Number of Taylor terms of φ₄ that reach rounding for an argument of norm ν: the least m with ν^m 4!/(m + 4)! ≤ eps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_taylor_terms"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_taylor_thresholds","page":"Internals","text":"Thresholds θ_m = (eps (m + 4)!/4!)^{1/m}, m = 1, 2, …, up to the first above PHI_TAYLOR_RADIUS: m terms reach rounding for norms up to θ_m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_taylor_thresholds"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.taylor_terms_from","page":"Internals","text":"Least m with ν ≤ thresholds[m], or the number of thresholds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.taylor_terms_from"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.taylor_coefficient","page":"Internals","text":"(c_j, c_{j-1}) of c_j = 4!/(j + 4)! from c = c_j for j ≤ m, and (0, c) for j > m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.taylor_coefficient"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi4_taylor","page":"Internals","text":"phi4_taylor(Z, m) -> P\n\nP = Σ_{j=0}^{m} Z^j 4!/(j + 4)!, so that φ₄(Z) ≈ P/24, by Paterson–Stockmeyer evaluation in blocks of four powers (Paterson & Stockmeyer 1973, SIAM J. Comput. 2, 60–66).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi4_taylor"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_double","page":"Internals","text":"phi_double(φ(Z)) -> φ(2Z)\n\nφ_k(2Z) = 2^{−k} [φ₀(Z) φ_k(Z) + Σ_{j=1}^{k} φ_j(Z) / (k − j)!] (Skaflestad & Wright 2009, Appl. Numer. Math. 59, 783–799).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_double"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_functions","page":"Internals","text":"phi_functions(A, scaling = balancing(A)) -> (φ₀, φ₁, φ₂, φ₃, φ₄)\n\nφ_k(A) = Σ_j A^j / (j + k)! for a 3×3 matrix A: with B = A .* to for (to, from) = scaling from balancing, a Taylor series of φ₄ at B / 2^s, with ‖B‖₁ / 2^s ≤ 4, followed by s doublings phi_double, and φ_k(A) = φ_k(B) .* from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phi_functions"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.exprb43_step","page":"Internals","text":"exprb43_step(rhs, t, u, h, F, J, v, scaling) -> (Δu, error)\n\nIncrement Δu of one exprb43 step of length h for u′ = rhs(t, u) from (t, u), with F = rhs(t, u), the Jacobian J = ∂rhs/∂u, v = ∂rhs/∂t at (t, u), and scaling = balancing(J). error is the difference from the embedded third-order solution, h φ₄(hJ) (12 D₃ − 48 D₂).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.exprb43_step"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Reference-solutions","page":"Validation","text":"The test suite solves the parcel model a second time with an independent integrator, test/reference/parcel_ode.jl: Radau IIA of order 5 with a simplified Newton iteration on a central-difference Jacobian, step-size control by step doubling, and events located by bisection on the step map. It has two models.\n\nFrozen-linear: the state (delta q_l q_i) under the coefficients of the problem at the start of the step. This is the problem MM2015FixedT solves exactly and MM2015PiecewiseLinear approximates.\nNonlinear: the parcel model in the state (q_l q_i T), with the saturation humidities, latent heats, heat capacity, and density following the state. This is the problem MM2015 solves.\n\nThe reference shares with the package only its thermodynamic functions; the frozen-linear model also takes the coefficients. Depletion times are checked against roots computed in 256- and 512-bit arithmetic.","title":"Reference solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#What-the-tests-check","page":"Validation","text":"MM2015FixedT against the frozen-linear reference: mean rates within 10^-10 of the larger rate (10^-12 measured on most states), and the same events at times within 10^-10 relative.\nMM2015 against the nonlinear reference at a relative tolerance of 10^-10: mean rates within ten times the tolerance, the same events at times within 10^-7 relative, and the supersaturations at the end of the step; in both moisture bases, with both thermodynamics backends, and in Float32 within ten times its default tolerance.\nThe exprb43 step at order 4 with fixed steps; the analytic Jacobian against finite differences; the varphi-functions against 512-bit values, within 8 times their componentwise condition number plus varepsilon.\nDepletion times over randomized inputs and near-tangent roots: within 10^-13 relative, or, where the root is ill-conditioned, with a residual within 64varepsilon of the size of the terms (the largest measured error is 31varepsilon); and the Lambert W functions at their piece boundaries.\nCoefficients built from host thermodynamics through ThermodynamicInputs equal those of the backend on every test state, in both moisture bases. In Float32, the ice rate from a small delta_i given with the coefficients stays within 64varepsilon of a 256-bit value.\nThresholds: condensate below x_min returns to the vapor without an exhaustion event, and supersaturations below δ_min and δ_i_min are set to zero. A parcel without condensate that stays subsaturated has zero rates in a single segment.\nInvariants on every test state and scheme: no phase loses more mass than it holds, a phase without mass never loses mass, and ice does not grow above the triple point in a step that does not cross it.\nZero allocations and inferred return types for every scheme, moisture basis, floating-point type, and backend.","title":"What the tests check"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Dependence-on-the-step","page":"Validation","text":"The Gallery shows the change over one step of each scheme and of the reference for steps from 1 s to an hour, in five regimes and under three forcings, with the difference of each scheme from the reference.","title":"Dependence on the step"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Accuracy","page":"Validation","text":"(Image: Error against the reference)\n\nLeft: relative error of the mean rates against the reference for the state between ice and liquid saturation of the Gallery, for steps from 1 s to an hour. MM2015 stays between 2times10^-11 and 6times10^-10, MM2015FixedT between 11times10^-4 and 11times10^-3, and MM2015PiecewiseLinear between 96times10^-4 and 0.28. Right: the error of MM2015 against its relative tolerance for three of the Gallery states, each over the step of its evolution figure, with the line where the error equals the tolerance.","title":"Accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Depletion-times","page":"Validation","text":"(Image: Error of the depletion times)\n\nRelative error of the exhaustion time of MM2015FixedT, in units of varepsilon, against 256-bit roots, over the initial supersaturation delta_0 and forcing A_c in units of the condensate. Gray marks where the condensate is not exhausted. The largest error on the grid is below 8varepsilon.","title":"Depletion times"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Test-states","page":"Validation","text":"Name T [K] p [hPa] humidity q_l, q_i [g kg⁻¹] tau_l, tau_i [s] forcing Delta t [s]\nwarm_updraft 285 900 r_vr_sl = 1.001 0.2, 0 5, 1000 w = 1 m s⁻¹ 60\nwarm_evaporation 290 950 r_vr_sl = 0.9 0.01, 0 10, 1000 — 120\nwbf 261 800 r_vr_sl = 0.995 0.2, 0.1 8, 60 w = 0.5 m s⁻¹ 300\nice_subliming_liquid_growing 275 850 r_vr_sl = 1.0005 0.1, 0.05 10, 20 — 120\nice_only_supersaturated 225 250 r_vr_si = 1.2 0, 0.01 1000, 600 w = 0.2 m s⁻¹ 600\nactivation_moistening 280 900 r_vr_sl = 0.999 0, 0 5, 1000 dot q_v = 2 mg kg⁻¹ s⁻¹ 30\nactivation_ascent 280 900 r_vr_sl = 0.999 0, 0 5, 1000 w = 1 m s⁻¹ 30\nactivation_cooling 280 900 r_vr_sl = 0.999 0, 0 5, 1000 dot T = −1 mK s⁻¹ 30\nfreezing_level 273.4 700 r_vr_sl = 1 0.1, 0.01 10, 100 dot T = −2 mK s⁻¹ 300\nstiff 280 900 r_vr_sl = 1.002 0.5, 0 0.1, 1000 w = 2 m s⁻¹ 60\nsluggish 280 900 r_vr_sl = 1.01 0.001, 0 10⁴, 10⁴ — 10\ndual_depletion 265 800 r_vr_sl = 0.8 0.001, 0.001 5, 20 — 120\nband 261 800 delta = −10⁻⁷ 0, 0.1 10, 50 ascent in the band 60\nmassless_ice_wbf 261.81 800 halfway between saturations 0.002, 0 10, 80 — 10\nice_subliming_above_freezing 276.77 900 r_vr_sl = 0.98 0, 0.7 6000, 4 — 10\nfloat_scale_near_freeze 269.4 882.68 delta = −1.6·10⁻⁵ 10⁻⁷, 4·10⁻⁸ 80, 5·10⁵ dot T, dot q_v 10\nfloat_scale_dual_depletion 270.2 882.68 delta_i = −2.4·10⁻⁵ 10⁻⁷, 10⁻⁷ 80, 5·10⁵ dot T, dot q_v 10\n\nHumidities are ratios of the vapor mixing ratio to the saturation mixing ratio over liquid (r_sl) or ice (r_si), or supersaturations in kg kg⁻¹. band rises at the speed that places it in a narrow band of ascent in which the ice holds the air just below liquid saturation, so no liquid forms. The two float_scale states warm at 0.28 mK s⁻¹ and moisten at 3.6 mg kg⁻¹ s⁻¹.","title":"Test states"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/t_updating/#MM2015","page":"MM2015","text":"MM2015 solves the parcel model itself. Nothing is frozen: the saturation humidities, Gamma_l, Gamma_i, alpha, Delta, the latent heats, the heat capacity, and the density follow T, p, and the humidities along the trajectory. Latent heating enters the temperature equation with L_v(T) and L_s(T).","title":"MM2015"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/t_updating/#Method","page":"MM2015","text":"The state u = (q_l q_i T) is integrated by the fourth-order exponential Rosenbrock method described on the Integrator page, with step-size control from its embedded third-order solution. The relaxation times enter through the matrix exponential of the Jacobian, so steps are limited by the change of the thermodynamic coefficients along the trajectory, and a relaxation time much shorter than Delta t costs no extra steps.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/t_updating/#Events","page":"MM2015","text":"The active phases follow the rule in Active phases and events. Liquid saturation, ice saturation, liquid exhaustion, and ice exhaustion are the events of MM2015FixedT. Because T changes, one more event exists: the crossing of the triple point T = T_mathrmtr, where the ice rules change. The integrator stops at every event: its time is the root, along the numerical solution, of the corresponding event function, located by a bracketing root finder.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/t_updating/#Keywords","page":"MM2015","text":"MM2015{FT}(; rtol, atol_q, atol_T, max_steps, root_finder, thresholds); MM2015() is MM2015{Float64}().\n\nKeyword Meaning Default\nrtol relative tolerance on q_l, q_i, T sqrtvarepsilon of FT\natol_q absolute tolerance on q_l and q_i [kg kg⁻¹] 10^-4 rtol\natol_T absolute tolerance on T [K] 100 rtol\nmax_steps largest number of integrator steps; more raise an error 10 000\nroot_finder bracketing root finder for events BrentRootFinder()\nthresholds condensate and supersaturations rounded at each step start (Integrator) Thresholds(), no rounding\n\nRootSolversRootFinder(method), after using RootSolvers, locates events with a bracketing method of RootSolvers.jl such as RootSolvers.BrentsMethod. An rtol below 4varepsilon of the problem's floating-point type raises an error, so a Float32 problem takes MM2015{Float32}().\n\nWith the defaults, on the 17 test states (default backend, specific humidity), the mean rates differ from a Radau IIA reference solved to a relative tolerance of 10^-13 by at most 15times10^-8 of the larger rate in Float64 (10^-11 to 10^-9 on most states) and 13times10^-4 in Float32. Larger tolerances take fewer steps.","title":"Keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/t_updating/#Relation-to-MM2015FixedT","page":"MM2015","text":"Suppose a backend's saturation curves are linear in T and p with equal liquid and ice slopes, and its latent heats, heat capacity, and density are constant. Then the frozen coefficients are exact, and MM2015 and MM2015FixedT give the same result to within the tolerance. For real thermodynamics the two differ whenever T changes during the step.","title":"Relation to MM2015FixedT"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Problem","page":"API","text":"","title":"Problem"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Schemes","page":"API","text":"","title":"Schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Rates","page":"API","text":"tendencies returns a named tuple with .liq and .ice fields, in kg kg⁻¹ s⁻¹ in the problem's moisture basis. For example, rates = tendencies(scheme, problem, Δt) gives rates.liq for condensation or evaporation and rates.ice for deposition or sublimation. The recorded step exposes the same named rates as trajectory(scheme, problem, Δt).rates.","title":"Rates"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Thermodynamics-from-the-host-model","page":"API","text":"A host model that already holds the saturation humidities, latent heats, heat capacity, and density of the parcel passes them as ThermodynamicInputs to coefficients and calls tendencies(scheme, k::Coefficients, Δt) with MM2015FixedT or MM2015PiecewiseLinear.","title":"Thermodynamics from the host model"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Recorded-steps","page":"API","text":"","title":"Recorded steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Root-finders","page":"API","text":"The keyword root_finder of MM2015 takes one of these.","title":"Root finders"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Thermodynamics-backends","page":"API","text":"A backend is DefaultThermodynamicsBackend or, after using Thermodynamics, any Thermodynamics.Parameters.AbstractThermodynamicsParameters set. A new backend implements the constants R_d, R_v, cp_d, cp_v, cp_l, cp_i, grav, and T_triple, and the functions saturation_vapor_pressure and latent_heat. The package derives everything else from these.","title":"Thermodynamics backends"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Figures","page":"API","text":"These functions have methods after using CairoMakie.","title":"Figures"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MorrisonMilbrandt2015","page":"API","text":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015\n\nCondensation, evaporation, deposition, and sublimation of a homogeneous air parcel over a time step, after Morrison & Milbrandt (2015), Appendix C. tendencies returns the mean rates of an MM2015Problem with one of three schemes:\n\nMM2015PiecewiseLinear: forward-Euler segments with rates frozen at each segment start.\nMM2015FixedT: the exact solution of the frozen-coefficient problem.\nMM2015: the parcel model solved numerically, with temperature evolving.\n\nThermodynamics.jl parameter sets serve as backends through a package extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MorrisonMilbrandt2015"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015Problem","page":"API","text":"MM2015Problem(basis, thermo, state, timescales, forcing)\n\nHomogeneous parcel problem: moisture basis, thermodynamics backend, state, relaxation times, and external forcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015Problem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015State","page":"API","text":"MM2015State(T, p, x_tot, x_liq, x_ice)\n\nParcel temperature [K], pressure [Pa], and total water, liquid, and ice in the moisture basis of the problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015State"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015Timescales","page":"API","text":"MM2015Timescales(τ_liq, τ_ice)\n\nSupersaturation relaxation times of liquid and of ice [s].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015Timescales"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015Forcing","page":"API","text":"MM2015Forcing(dpdt, dTdt_external, dx_vap_dt)\n\nExternal forcing, constant over the step: pressure tendency [Pa s⁻¹], temperature tendency from radiation and mixing [K s⁻¹], and vapor tendency from mixing [s⁻¹] in the moisture basis of the problem. A hydrostatic parcel rising at speed w has dpdt = -ρ g w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015Forcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.AbstractMoistureBasis","page":"API","text":"Mass normalization of the moisture variables of an MM2015Problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.AbstractMoistureBasis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.SpecificHumidity","page":"API","text":"Water mass per unit moist-air mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.SpecificHumidity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.DryAirMixingRatio","page":"API","text":"Water mass per unit dry-air mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.DryAirMixingRatio"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015PiecewiseLinear","page":"API","text":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear(; thresholds = Thresholds())\n\nForward-Euler step of Appendix C from each event to the next, with rates frozen at the start of every segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015PiecewiseLinear"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015FixedT","page":"API","text":"MM2015FixedT(; thresholds = Thresholds())\n\nAppendix C of Morrison & Milbrandt (2015): the parcel model with every coefficient frozen at the start of the step, solved exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015FixedT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015","page":"API","text":"MM2015{FT}(; rtol, atol_q, atol_T, max_steps, root_finder, thresholds)\nMM2015(; rtol, atol_q, atol_T, max_steps, root_finder, thresholds)\n\nThe parcel model solved by an exponential Rosenbrock integrator to relative tolerance rtol (default √eps(FT)), absolute tolerance atol_q [kg kg⁻¹] on liquid and ice (default 10⁻⁴ rtol), and absolute tolerance atol_T [K] on temperature (default 100 rtol), in at most max_steps steps, with events bracketed by root_finder and the state rounded by Thresholds thresholds. MM2015() is MM2015{Float64}().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MM2015"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Thresholds","page":"API","text":"Thresholds(; x_min = 0, δ_min = 0, δ_i_min = 0)\n\nMagnitudes below which a scheme rounds the state to its limit, in the moisture basis of the problem [kg kg⁻¹]: liquid or ice below x_min counts as absent and returns to the vapor during the step, and a supersaturation over liquid below δ_min or over ice below δ_i_min in magnitude counts as saturation. The defaults round nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Thresholds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.tendencies","page":"API","text":"tendencies(scheme, problem, Δt) -> (; liq, ice)\ntendencies(scheme, k::Coefficients, Δt) -> (; liq, ice)\n\nMean phase-change rates of liquid and ice over the step Δt [s], in the moisture basis of problem [kg kg⁻¹ s⁻¹]. The frozen-coefficient schemes also take Coefficients, for example built from the host model's thermodynamics by coefficients. Δt = 0 gives (; liq = 0, ice = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.tendencies"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.validate","page":"API","text":"validate(problem, Δt)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError when problem or the step Δt lies outside the domain of the schemes. tendencies does not call it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.validate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Coefficients","page":"API","text":"Coefficients(T, p, x_l, x_i, x_sl, x_si, δ, δ_i, Γ_l, Γ_i, α, τ_l, τ_i, A_l, below_triple)\n\nAppendix C quantities of the parcel model at one state, in the moisture basis of the problem: temperature T [K], pressure p [Pa], liquid x_l and ice x_i, saturation humidities x_sl and x_si, the supersaturations δ = x_v − x_sl and δ_i = x_v − x_si, the factors Γ_l, Γ_i, α, the relaxation times τ_l and τ_i [s], the external forcing A_l [s⁻¹] of δ, and whether the parcel counts as below the triple point. The constructor adds Δ = x_sl − x_si, the rates r_l = 1/(τ_l Γ_l), r_i = 1/(τ_i Γ_i), k_l = 1/τ_l, k_i = α r_i [s⁻¹], k_lΔ = k_l Δ, k_iΔ = k_i Δ, and the relaxation times τ_ice_only = 1/k_i and τ_both = 1/(k_l + k_i) [s].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Coefficients"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.coefficients","page":"API","text":"coefficients(problem) -> Coefficients\ncoefficients(basis, state, timescales, forcing, inputs::ThermodynamicInputs; δ, δ_i) -> Coefficients\n\nCoefficients at the state of problem, or of a state whose thermodynamics the host model supplies in inputs. The supersaturations over liquid δ and over ice δ_i [kg kg⁻¹] default to x_v − x_sl and x_v − x_si from the state; a host model that holds them passes them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.coefficients"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.ThermodynamicInputs","page":"API","text":"ThermodynamicInputs(; x_sl, x_si, dx_sl_dT, dx_si_dT, e_sl, L_v, L_s, c_pm, ρ, T_triple)\n\nThermodynamics of the parcel at the start of a step, from the host model, in the moisture basis of the problem:\n\nx_sl, x_si: saturation humidities over liquid and ice [kg kg⁻¹] at the parcel's temperature and pressure, x_s = q_d ε e_s/(p − e_s) in specific humidity and ε e_s/(p − e_s) in dry-air mixing ratio, with ε = R_d/R_v and q_d the dry-air fraction;\ndx_sl_dT, dx_si_dT: their temperature derivatives at constant pressure and total water [kg kg⁻¹ K⁻¹], x_s p L/((p − e_s) R_v T²) with L the latent heat of the phase;\ne_sl: saturation vapor pressure over liquid [Pa];\nL_v, L_s: latent heats of vaporization and sublimation [J kg⁻¹];\nc_pm: moist-air isobaric heat capacity [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹];\nρ: moist-air density [kg m⁻³];\nT_triple: triple-point temperature [K].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.ThermodynamicInputs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.thermodynamic_inputs","page":"API","text":"thermodynamic_inputs(problem) -> ThermodynamicInputs\n\nThermodynamicInputs of problem from its thermodynamics backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.thermodynamic_inputs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.trajectory","page":"API","text":"trajectory(scheme, problem, Δt)\ntrajectory(scheme, k::Coefficients, Δt)\n\nThe step of tendencies with its segments recorded in a Trajectory: the segments between events of a frozen-coefficient scheme, the integrator steps of MM2015.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.trajectory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Trajectory","page":"API","text":"Trajectory{FT, S, C}\n\nA step of scheme with its segments: the scheme, the context it evaluates states with (the Coefficients of a frozen-coefficient scheme, the problem of MM2015), the step Δt [s], the segments, and the mean rates of liquid and ice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Trajectory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Segment","page":"API","text":"Segment{FT}\n\nOne segment of a step: start time t [s], duration [s], the active phases, the state at its start (liquid x_l, ice x_i, temperature T, supersaturations δ and δ_i), and the event that ends it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.state_at","page":"API","text":"state_at(trajectory, t) -> (; δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i, T)\n\nSupersaturations over liquid and over ice, liquid, ice, and temperature at time t in [0, Δt] of a recorded step.\n\n\n\n\n\nstate_at(trajectory::Trajectory{FT, <:MM2015}, t) -> (; δ, δ_i, x_l, x_i, T)\n\nSupersaturation over liquid and over ice, liquid, ice, and temperature at time t in [0, Δt], on the step map of the integrator step that contains t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.state_at"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.EventKind","page":"API","text":"Event that ends a segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.EventKind"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.ActivePhases","page":"API","text":"Phases that exchange mass with the vapor during a segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.ActivePhases"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.AbstractRootFinder","page":"API","text":"Root finder that brackets the events of MM2015.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.AbstractRootFinder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.BrentRootFinder","page":"API","text":"Brent–Dekker iteration with a bisection safeguard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.BrentRootFinder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.RootSolversRootFinder","page":"API","text":"RootSolversRootFinder(method)\n\nRoot finder of RootSolvers.jl with the bracketing method method, a type such as RootSolvers.BrentsMethod. Requires using RootSolvers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.RootSolversRootFinder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.bracket_root","page":"API","text":"bracket_root(finder, f, lo, hi, f_lo, f_hi, tolerance) -> (lo, hi)\n\nBracket around a sign change of f, from f(lo) = f_lo ≤ 0 and f(hi) = f_hi > 0, of width at most tolerance + 4 eps max(|lo|, |hi|). The returned points keep those signs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.bracket_root"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.DefaultThermodynamicsBackend","page":"API","text":"DefaultThermodynamicsBackend()\n\nThermodynamics backend without dependencies, with fixed gas constants, heat capacities, gravity, and triple point. Its saturation vapor pressures integrate the Clausius–Clapeyron relation with latent heats that vary linearly with temperature (Kirchhoff), anchored at the triple point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.DefaultThermodynamicsBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.AbstractPhase","page":"API","text":"Condensed phase of water.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.AbstractPhase"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Liquid","page":"API","text":"Liquid water; saturation over a plane liquid surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Liquid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Ice","page":"API","text":"Ice; saturation over a plane ice surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.Ice"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation_vapor_pressure","page":"API","text":"saturation_vapor_pressure(thermo, T, phase)\n\nSaturation vapor pressure over a plane surface of phase (Liquid or Ice) [Pa] at temperature T [K]. A backend must satisfy d ln e/dT = L(T) / (R_v T²) with its own latent_heat, and give equal liquid and ice values at T_triple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation_vapor_pressure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.latent_heat","page":"API","text":"latent_heat(thermo, T, phase)\n\nLatent heat of the transition from vapor to phase [J kg⁻¹] at temperature T [K]: vaporization for Liquid, sublimation for Ice. A backend must satisfy dL/dT = cp_v − c, with c its heat capacity of phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.latent_heat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.R_d","page":"API","text":"Dry-air gas constant [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹]: R_d(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.R_d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.R_v","page":"API","text":"Water-vapor gas constant [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹]: R_v(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.R_v"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_d","page":"API","text":"Isobaric heat capacity of dry air [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹]: cp_d(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_v","page":"API","text":"Isobaric heat capacity of water vapor [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹]: cp_v(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_v"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_l","page":"API","text":"Heat capacity of liquid water [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹]: cp_l(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_l"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_i","page":"API","text":"Heat capacity of ice [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹]: cp_i(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_i"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.grav","page":"API","text":"Gravitational acceleration [m s⁻²]: grav(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.grav"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.T_triple","page":"API","text":"Triple-point temperature of water [K]: T_triple(thermo, FT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.T_triple"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation","page":"API","text":"saturation(thermo, T, p, phase) -> PhaseSaturation\n\nPhaseSaturation over phase at temperature T [K] and pressure p [Pa]. The temperature derivatives follow from the backend's Clausius–Clapeyron and Kirchhoff relations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.saturation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.PhaseSaturation","page":"API","text":"PhaseSaturation{FT}\n\nSaturation over one condensed phase at (T, p): vapor pressure e [Pa], latent heat L [J kg⁻¹] and dL_dT, and the saturation mixing ratio r = ε e / (p − e) [kg kg⁻¹ of dry air] with dr_dT, d2r_dT2, dr_dp, and d2r_dTdp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.PhaseSaturation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.heat_capacity","page":"API","text":"Heat capacity of phase [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.heat_capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_m","page":"API","text":"cp_m(thermo, q_t, q_l, q_i)\n\nIsobaric heat capacity of moist air [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹] per unit moist-air mass, from specific humidities.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.cp_m"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.gas_constant_air","page":"API","text":"gas_constant_air(thermo, q_t, q_l, q_i)\n\nGas constant of moist air [J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹], R_d (1 − q_t) + R_v q_v, from specific humidities.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.gas_constant_air"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.air_density","page":"API","text":"air_density(thermo, T, p, q_t, q_l, q_i)\n\nDensity of moist air [kg m⁻³] from the ideal-gas law, from specific humidities.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.air_density"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_step_change","page":"API","text":"plot_step_change(problem, schemes, Δts; labels, reference = nothing) -> Figure\n\nChange of liquid, ice, and their sum over one step of each of schemes for problem, against the step Δt for each step in Δts [s], on a symmetric logarithmic axis, with two guides: the change at the rate of the start of the step, and the loss of all of a phase. Color marks the phase and line style the scheme. reference = (; Δts, changes) adds the liquid and ice changes changes of a reference solution at its steps Δts as dots, and a panel with the difference of each scheme from it relative to the larger reference change. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_step_change"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_step_change!","page":"API","text":"plot_step_change!(position, problem, schemes, Δts; labels, reference = nothing, difference = true, ylimits = nothing) -> (; change, difference)\n\nplot_step_change without its legend into the figure position position, returning the axes. The difference panel is drawn only when reference is given and difference holds; without it, the returned difference is nothing. ylimits = (lo, hi) sets the range of the change axis [kg kg⁻¹]. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_step_change!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_evolution","page":"API","text":"plot_evolution(trajectories; labels, panels = (:condensate, :supersaturation, :temperature)) -> Figure\n\nLiquid and ice (:condensate), the supersaturations over liquid and over ice (:supersaturation), and the temperature (:temperature) of each trajectory of one problem against time over its step, one panel per entry of panels, with a marker at each event. Color marks the phase and line style the trajectory. A phase that holds no mass and never exchanges any is left out. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_evolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_evolution!","page":"API","text":"plot_evolution!(position, trajectories; labels, panels) -> NamedTuple of axes\n\nplot_evolution without its legend into the figure position position, with the panels side by side, returning the axes by panel. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.plot_evolution!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.figure_theme","page":"API","text":"figure_theme() -> Makie.Theme\n\nTheme of the package figures: chart surface, hairline grid and axes, and text in ink. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.figure_theme"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.series_color","page":"API","text":"series_color(i) -> color\n\nColor of the i-th series of the package figures, from a palette of eight in a fixed order. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.series_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phase_color","page":"API","text":"phase_color(phase) -> color\n\nColor of liquid (:liquid), ice (:ice), or their sum (:total) in the package figures. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.phase_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.series_style","page":"API","text":"series_style(i) -> (linestyle, linewidth)\n\nLine style and width of the i-th scheme or trajectory of the package figures, for i up to 4. Requires using CairoMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.series_style"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#MM2015FixedT","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"MM2015FixedT() is Appendix C of Morrison & Milbrandt (2015): it solves the parcel model with every coefficient frozen at its value at the start of the step (derivation). Within the step it is exact for that frozen-coefficient problem.","title":"MM2015FixedT"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#Dynamics","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"The scheme carries the supersaturations over liquid, delta, and over ice, delta_i, as two states. With Delta fixed, delta_i = delta + Delta and both change at the rate\n\nfracddeltadt = fracddelta_idt = f(delta) = A_l - lfracdeltatau_l\n  - ialphafracdelta_itau_iGamma_i\n\nwhich is C1 with the inactive phase's term removed from C2 and C4. The same equation serves liquid only, ice only, and both phases.","title":"Dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#Segments","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"Between events the set of active phases is fixed, and both supersaturations relax at the rate\n\ntau^-1 = fracltau_l + ifracalphatau_iGamma_i\n\ntoward their own equilibria:\n\nfracddeltadt = A_c - fracdeltatau quad A_c = A_l - ifracDeltaalphatau_iGamma_i\nqquad\nfracddelta_idt = A_ci - fracdelta_itau quad A_ci = A_l + lfracDeltatau_l\n\nThe segment solution is C5 for each. The liquid increment integrates S_l = ldelta(tau_lGamma_l) and the ice increment integrates S_i = idelta_i(tau_iGamma_i), which gives C6 and C7 (Frozen coefficients). Near ice saturation delta_i is small against delta and Delta; carried as its own state, it keeps its relative precision. With neither phase active, tau = infty and both supersaturations change linearly at the rate A_l.","title":"Segments"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#Events","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"A segment ends at the earliest of the following times, or at the end of the step.\n\nEvent Condition Time from the segment start\nliquid saturation delta = 0 taulndfracdelta_0 - A_ctau-A_ctau\nice saturation delta_i = 0 taulndfracdelta_i0 - A_citau-A_citau\nliquid exhausted q_l(t) = 0 smallest positive root, closed form (Depletion)\nice exhausted q_i(t) = 0 smallest positive root, closed form (Depletion)\n\nA saturation time exists when the argument of the logarithm exceeds 1; with tau = infty it is -delta_0A_l (-delta_i0A_l for ice) when positive. With x the supersaturation of the phase (delta for liquid, delta_i for ice) and A its forcing (A_c or A_ci), the exhaustion time is the smallest positive t with\n\nAt + (x_0 - Atau)left(1 - e^-ttauright) = -fracq_c0tau_cGamma_ctau\n\nAt an event, the crossed quantity is set to its boundary value, the active phases are re-evaluated by the rule in Active phases and events, and the next segment starts from that state. The same page shows that a step has at most eight events, and states the thresholds and the exit for clear air that apply at each segment start.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#Coefficients-from-the-host-model","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"The scheme reads the problem only through its Coefficients. A host model that already holds the saturation humidities, latent heats, heat capacity, and density passes them to coefficients as ThermodynamicInputs, together with delta and delta_i when it holds them more precisely than the difference of the vapor and the saturation humidity, and calls tendencies(MM2015FixedT(), k, Δt).\n\nThe coefficients split the change of the saturation humidity into its temperature, pressure, and total-water parts, so the temperature derivatives are taken at constant pressure and total water, and the pressure part follows as partial_p x_sl = -x_sl(p - e_sl). A derivative of q_s = e_s(rho R_v T) at constant density is smaller by the factor (1 - R_v TL)(p - e_s)p, 4 to 8 % on the test states, and with it the rates of the test states change by up to 11 %.","title":"Coefficients from the host model"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#Result","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"The mean rates are the summed condensate increments of all segments divided by Delta t.","title":"Result"},{"category":"section","location":"schemes/fixed_T/#Accuracy","page":"MM2015FixedT","text":"The result is exact for the frozen-coefficient problem. Its difference from the parcel model comes from the frozen coefficients alone, listed in What the frozen coefficients leave out.","title":"Accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/appendix_c/#Appendix-C-of-Morrison-and-Milbrandt-(2015)","page":"Appendix C","text":"Morrison, H., and J. A. Milbrandt, 2015: Parameterization of cloud microphysics based on the prediction of bulk ice particle properties. Part I: Scheme description and idealized tests. J. Atmos. Sci., 72, 287–311, Appendix C, section b.","title":"Appendix C of Morrison & Milbrandt (2015)"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/appendix_c/#Published-equations","page":"Appendix C","text":"The paper writes mixing ratios: q is the vapor mixing ratio, q_sl and q_si the saturation mixing ratios over liquid and ice, and delta = q - q_sl.\n\nfracddeltadt = fracdqdt - fracdq_sldTfracdTdt = A_c - fracdeltatau\ntagC1\n\ntau^-1 = tau_c^-1 + tau_r^-1\n  + left(1 + fracL_sc_pfracdq_sldTright)fractau_i^-1Gamma_i\ntagC2\n\nGamma_i = 1 + fracL_sc_pfracdq_sidT\ntagC3\n\nA_c = left(fracpartial qpartial tright)_mathrmmix\n  - fracq_slrho g wp - e_s\n  - fracdq_sldTleftleft(fracpartial Tpartial tright)_mathrmrad\n  + left(fracpartial Tpartial tright)_mathrmmix - fracw gc_pright\n  - fracq_sl - q_sitau_iGamma_lleft(1 + fracL_sc_pfracdq_sldTright)\ntagC4\n\ndelta(t) = A_ctau + (delta_t=0 - A_ctau)e^-ttau\ntagC5\n\nmathrmQCCON = A_cfractautau_cGamma_l\n  + (delta_t=0 - A_ctau)fractauDelta ttau_cGamma_lleft(1 - e^-Delta ttauright)\ntagC6\n\nmathrmQICON = A_cfractautau_iGamma_i\n  + (delta_t=0 - A_ctau)fractauDelta ttau_iGamma_ileft(1 - e^-Delta ttauright)\n  + fracq_sl - q_sitau_iGamma_i\ntagC7\n\nGamma_l is C3 with L_s to L_v and q_si to q_sl. The paper caps tau at 10^8 s when no hydrometeors are present. The rain rate is C6 with tau_c to tau_r; this package combines cloud and rain into one liquid category with tau_l^-1 = tau_c^-1 + tau_r^-1.","title":"Published equations"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/appendix_c/#Derivation-from-the-parcel-model","page":"Appendix C","text":"Differentiate delta = q_v - q_sl(T p q_t) along the parcel model and substitute its temperature equation. The terms free of the phase-change rates collect into the external forcing A_l, and the latent heating of the rates gives one factor per phase:\n\nfracddeltadt = A_l - Gamma_lS_l - alphaS_i\n\nA_l = F_v - partial_T q_slleft(dot T_mathrmext + fracdot prhoc_pmright)\n  - partial_p q_sldot p - partial_q_t q_slF_v \n\nThis identity holds at every instant; it involves no approximation. With dot p = -rho g w, the second and third terms of A_l are the second and third terms of C4. The last term exists only in specific humidity, because q_sl = (1 - q_t)r_sl depends on q_t and the saturation mixing ratio r_sl does not.\n\nInserting the rates S_l = ldelta(tau_lGamma_l) and S_i = i(delta + Delta)(tau_iGamma_i) gives C1 with\n\ntau^-1 = fracltau_l + ifracalphatau_iGamma_i qquad\nA_c = A_l - ifracDeltaalphatau_iGamma_i\n\nWith both phases active this is C2 and C4. For liquid, the Gamma_l of the rate and the Gamma_l from latent heating cancel, leaving 1tau_l. For ice, the rate carries 1Gamma_i and the latent heating carries alpha. C2 contains both factors, so in the paper the Gamma in each rate and the latent heating in dTdt are separate terms, and the parcel model keeps both.","title":"Derivation from the parcel model"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/appendix_c/#The-C4-denominator","page":"Appendix C","text":"The derivation gives tau_iGamma_i in the last term of C4; the paper prints tau_iGamma_l. The two agree only when Gamma_l = Gamma_i. C2 and C7 both use Gamma_i, and only Gamma_i reproduces C1. This package uses Gamma_i.\n\nThe check below compares ddeltadt of the full parcel model, computed by a centered difference along its trajectory, against A_c - deltatau from the derived coefficients and from the printed C4. Thermodynamics.jl 1.3 supplies the thermodynamic functions.\n\nstate T [K] p [hPa] phases derived C4, relative error printed C4, relative error\nwarm updraft, w ≈ 1 m s⁻¹ 285 900 liquid −1.9e−9 −1.9e−9\nmixed phase 261 800 liquid + ice −4.1e−10 −1.2e−2\nmixed phase, moistening and cooling 261 800 liquid + ice −2.0e−10 −1.1e−2\nabove freezing 275 850 liquid + ice −2.0e−9 +1.6e−1\nice only, supersaturated over ice 225 250 ice +1.2e−9 +1.9e−2\n\nThe derived form agrees to the truncation error of the difference. The printed form is off by 1 to 16 % whenever ice is active.","title":"The C4 denominator"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/appendix_c/#Frozen-coefficients","page":"Appendix C","text":"Appendix C holds A_c, tau, Gamma_l, Gamma_i, alpha, and Delta at their values at the start of the interval. C1 is then linear with constant coefficients, and its solution is C5. The condensate follows by integrating the rates:\n\nq_l(t) = q_l0 + fracltau_lGamma_lleftA_ctaut\n  + (delta_0 - A_ctau)tauleft(1 - e^-ttauright)right\n\nq_i(t) = q_i0 + fracitau_iGamma_ileft(A_ctau + Delta)t\n  + (delta_0 - A_ctau)tauleft(1 - e^-ttauright)right\n\nDividing the increments by t = Delta t gives C6 and C7. The term Delta(tau_iGamma_i) in C7 converts the liquid-frame supersaturation to the ice supersaturation that drives deposition. With neither phase active, tau = infty and delta(t) = delta_0 + A_lt.","title":"Frozen coefficients"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/appendix_c/#What-the-frozen-coefficients-leave-out","page":"Appendix C","text":"Freezing the coefficients linearizes q_sl(T p) and q_si(T p) about the start of the interval and holds Gamma, L, c_pm, and rho fixed. It also holds Delta fixed, while the parcel model gives\n\nfracdDeltadt = (partial_T q_sl - partial_T q_si)fracdTdt\n  + (partial_p q_sl - partial_p q_si)dot p\n  + (partial_q_t q_sl - partial_q_t q_si)F_v \n\nKeeping Delta free turns the linearization into a coupled system for delta and delta_i:\n\nfracddtbeginpmatrixdelta delta_iendpmatrix\n= beginpmatrixA_l A_iendpmatrix\n- beginpmatrix ltau_l  ialpha(tau_iGamma_i) \n  lalpha(tau_lGamma_l)  itau_i endpmatrix\n  beginpmatrixdelta delta_iendpmatrix\nqquad alpha = 1 + fracL_vc_pmpartial_T q_si\n\nwhere A_i is A_l written with the ice saturation quantities. With both phases active, the determinant of the matrix is\n\nfracGamma_lGamma_i - alphaalphatau_ltau_iGamma_lGamma_i qquad\nGamma_lGamma_i - alphaalpha = -frac(L_s - L_v)(partial_T q_sl - partial_T q_si)c_pm \n\nBelow T_mathrmtr, partial_T q_sl  partial_T q_si, so the determinant is negative and one mode of the coupled linearization grows slowly: the fusion heat of the liquid-to-ice conversion raises T, which widens Delta. For the mixed-phase state in the table the two decay rates are 0.142 s⁻¹ and −1.5 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹; for the ice-only state, 1.7 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ and −1.5 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹. Above T_mathrmtr both are positive (0.150 s⁻¹ and 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ for the 275 K state).\n\nMM2015FixedT solves the frozen-coefficient problem exactly. MM2015 solves the parcel model itself and keeps every term above.","title":"What the frozen coefficients leave out"},{"category":"section","location":"depletion/#Condensate-exhaustion","page":"Depletion","text":"A phase holding condensate q_c under exponential relaxation is exhausted at the smallest positive root of\n\nf(t) = bar At + Kleft(1 - e^-ttauright) - c\nqquad K = delta_0 - A_ctau qquad c = -fracq_ctau_cGammatau\n\nwith bar A = A_c without WBF and bar A = A_c + (q_sl - q_si)tau with it. In hat u = ttau, with u = bar Atau and beta = tau f(0),\n\ng(hat u) = up(hat u) + betae(hat u) - c\nqquad e = 1 - e^-hat u qquad p = hat u - e ","title":"Condensate exhaustion"},{"category":"section","location":"depletion/#Root-structure","page":"Depletion","text":"g = -K e^-hat u has one sign, so g has at most one turning point and the roots follow from signs.\n\nIf u and K have equal signs, or beta is zero or has the sign of u, g is monotone on hat u  0 and a root exists exactly when c and u have equal signs.\nIf u and K have opposite signs and beta has the sign of K, g turns at hat u_star = -ln(1 - betaK)  0. With the depth D = g(hat u_star),\n\ng(hat u_star + s) = D + uleft(e^-s - 1 + sright)\n\nso the roots are hat u_star + s_pm, where e^-s - 1 + s = rho = -Du. No root exists when D has the sign of u. The first root lies before hat u_star when g(0) = -c has the sign of u, and after it when -c has the opposite sign. When D is below the rounding floor, the two roots coincide at hat u_star to working precision.","title":"Root structure"},{"category":"section","location":"depletion/#Seeds-and-certification","page":"Depletion","text":"Each root starts from a closed form:\n\ns_pm = 1 + rho + W_0-1left(-e^-1-rhoright), and its series s_pm = pm h + h^26 pm h^336 + h^4270 pm h^54320 in h = sqrt2rho near the turning point;\nhat u = s_0 + W_0left(pm e^Eright) with s_0 = (c - K)u and E = lnKu - s_0 in the monotone case, with the asymptotic series of W_0 where e^E overflows;\nthe smallest positive root of the quadratic Taylor model betahat u - Khat u^22 - c when its cubic truncation error is below sqrtvarepsilon.\n\nNewton's method on g polishes the seed until g le 32varepsilon(up + betae + c), with p and e evaluated to a few ulps. An iterate that leaves the bracket (0 hat u_star), (hat u_star s_0), (0 s_0) or (0 infty) restarts at the bracket end where gg  0, from which Newton converges monotonically; g(s_0) = -K e^-s_0 makes s_0 such an end. One more Newton correction is applied when the Kantorovich condition gg le g^22 holds. A root that does not reach the floor within MAX_DEPLETION_CORRECTIONS corrections raises an error.\n\nMM2015FixedT uses these depletion times. MM2015 locates exhaustion as an event along its numerical solution (Integrator).","title":"Seeds and certification"},{"category":"section","location":"depletion/#Functions","page":"Depletion","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.get_t_out_of_q_no_WBF","page":"Depletion","text":"get_t_out_of_q_no_WBF(δ_0, A_c, τ, τ_c, q_c, Γ) -> FT\n\nSmallest positive time at which condensate q_c is exhausted under exponential (no-WBF) relaxation, or Inf if the phase never exhausts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.get_t_out_of_q_no_WBF"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.get_t_out_of_q_WBF","page":"Depletion","text":"get_t_out_of_q_WBF(δ_0, A_c, τ, τ_c, q_ice, Γ, q_sl, q_si) -> FT\n\nSmallest positive time at which ice q_ice is exhausted when its rate includes the C7 term in q_sl − q_si, or Inf if the ice never exhausts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.get_t_out_of_q_WBF"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015._mm_smallest_depletion_time","page":"Depletion","text":"_mm_smallest_depletion_time(Ā, K, τ, c, β) -> FT\n\nSmallest positive root of f(t) = Ā·t + K·(1 − e^{−t/τ}) − c, or Inf if none, with β = τ·f′(0) taken from the caller's inputs (δ_0, or δ_0 + Bτ with WBF). The root is certified by its residual: |g| ≤ g_floor from _mm_depl_eval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015._mm_smallest_depletion_time"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015._mm_depl_eval","page":"Depletion","text":"_mm_depl_eval(û, u, K, c, β) -> (g, g′, g″, g_floor)\n\ng(û) = u·p + β·e − c, g′(û) = β − K·e, g″(û) = K·(e − 1), and g_floor = 32ε·(|u·p| + |β·e| + |c|), below which g is zero to working precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015._mm_depl_eval"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015._mm_depl_newton","page":"Depletion","text":"_mm_depl_newton(û, lo, hi, restart, u, K, c, β) -> FT\n\nNewton corrections of the closed-form seed û of the root of g in (lo, hi), stopped at the rounding floor, at most MAX_DEPLETION_CORRECTIONS. An iterate outside (lo, hi) is replaced by restart, a point where g·g″ > 0, from which Newton converges monotonically to the root. The first iterate within the floor receives one more Newton correction when the Kantorovich condition |g″|·|g| ≤ g′²/2 holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015._mm_depl_newton"},{"category":"constant","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MAX_DEPLETION_CORRECTIONS","page":"Depletion","text":"Largest number of Newton corrections applied to a closed-form depletion seed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.MAX_DEPLETION_CORRECTIONS"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015._depletion_quadratic_seed","page":"Depletion","text":"_depletion_quadratic_seed(K, c, β) -> FT\n\nSmallest positive root of the quadratic model β·û − (K/2)·û² − c of g when its cubic truncation error is below √ε relative, else NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015._depletion_quadratic_seed"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015._depletion_phi_inverse","page":"Depletion","text":"_depletion_phi_inverse(ρ, σ) -> FT\n\nSolution s of e^{−s} − 1 + s = ρ with sign(s) = σ = ±1, for ρ > 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015._depletion_phi_inverse"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015._depletion_pe","page":"Depletion","text":"_depletion_pe(û) -> (p, e)\n\np = û − (1 − e^{−û}) and e = 1 − e^{−û} for û ≥ 0, each with relative error below 4ε.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015._depletion_pe"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.fast_lambertw0","page":"Depletion","text":"fast_lambertw0(z)\n\nPrincipal real branch W₀(z) for z ≥ -1/e: -1 at -1/e, NaN below it. Float16 and Float32 arguments are evaluated in Float64; other floating-point types iterate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.fast_lambertw0"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.fast_lambertwm1","page":"Depletion","text":"fast_lambertwm1(z)\n\nLower real branch W₋₁(z) for -1/e ≤ z < 0: -1 at -1/e, -Inf at 0, NaN elsewhere. Float16 and Float32 arguments are evaluated in Float64; other floating-point types iterate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.fast_lambertwm1"},{"category":"function","location":"depletion/#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.fast_lambertwm1_from_ln","page":"Depletion","text":"fast_lambertwm1_from_ln(lnmz)\n\nW₋₁(z) for z = -e^{lnmz}, from lnmz = ln(-z) ≤ -1. Float64 evaluates the branch-point piece in z + 1/e = -expm1(lnmz + 1)/e, the next pieces in z, and Fukushima's pieces in ln(-z) down to lnmz = -745, iterating on w + ln(-w) = lnmz below. Float16 and Float32 are evaluated in Float64; other floating-point types iterate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.fast_lambertwm1_from_ln"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Integrator","page":"Integrator","text":"MM2015 integrates the parcel model\n\nu = F(t u) qquad u = (q_l q_i T)\n\nwhere F is smooth between events because the set of active phases is fixed there. F depends on time explicitly through p(t) and q_t(t).","title":"Integrator"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Exponential-Rosenbrock-method","page":"Integrator","text":"The method is exprb43 of Hochbruck, Ostermann & Schweitzer (2009), SIAM J. Numer. Anal. 47, 786–803, doi:10.1137/080717717: fourth order, three stages, with an embedded third-order solution for step-size control. Each step linearizes F at (t_n u_n):\n\nJ_n = fracpartial Fpartial u(t_n u_n) qquad\nv_n = fracpartial Fpartial t(t_n u_n) qquad\ng_n(t u) = F(t u) - J_n u - v_n t\n\nand uses the entire functions\n\nvarphi_0(z) = e^z qquad\nvarphi_k(z) = int_0^1 e^(1-sigma)zfracsigma^k-1(k-1)dsigma qquad\nvarphi_k(z) = fracvarphi_k-1(z) - varphi_k-1(0)z\n\nIn the non-autonomous form (the paper's eqs. 6.5a and 6.5b) with step h,\n\nU_ni = u_n + c_i hvarphi_1(c_i h J_n)F(t_n u_n) + c_i^2 h^2varphi_2(c_i h J_n)v_n\n  + hsum_j=2^i-1 a_ij(h J_n)D_nj\n\nu_n+1 = u_n + hvarphi_1(h J_n)F(t_n u_n) + h^2varphi_2(h J_n)v_n\n  + hsum_i=2^3 b_i(h J_n)D_ni\nqquad D_nj = g_n(t_n + c_j h U_nj) - g_n(t_n u_n)\n\nThe exprb43 coefficients are c = (0 tfrac12 1), a_32 = varphi_1, and\n\n b_2 b_3\nfourth order 16varphi_3 - 48varphi_4 -2varphi_3 + 12varphi_4\nembedded third order 16varphi_3 -2varphi_3\n\nThe linear part J_n u is integrated exactly by the matrix functions, and it carries the relaxation rates 1tau_l and alpha(tau_iGamma_i). A relaxation time far shorter than the step therefore places no limit on the step.","title":"Exponential Rosenbrock method"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Jacobian","page":"Integrator","text":"The entries of J_n follow by differentiating the parcel model. With the liquid rate S_l = delta(tau_lGamma_l),\n\nfracpartial S_lpartial q_l = -frac1tau_lGamma_l - fracS_lGamma_lfracpartialGamma_lpartial q_l\nqquad\nfracpartial S_lpartial T = -fracpartial_T q_sltau_lGamma_l - fracS_lGamma_lpartial_TGamma_l\nqquad\npartial_TGamma_l = frac1c_pmleft(fracdL_vdTpartial_T q_sl + L_vpartial^2_T q_slright)\n\nand partialGamma_lpartial q_l = -(Gamma_l - 1)(c_l - c_pv)c_pm, because c_pm depends on the condensate. The ice entries are analogous. The temperature row differentiates dot p(rho c_pm) + dot T_mathrmext + (L_v S_l + L_s S_i)c_pm, including partialrhopartial T = -rhoT and partialrhopartial q_l = rho R_vR_m. The second derivative of the saturation humidity is\n\npartial^2_T q_sl = partial_T q_slleftbeta + frace_lp - e_l + fracL_vL_v - frac2Tright\nqquad beta = fracpp - e_lfracL_vR_v T^2 qquad e_l = e_lfracL_vR_v T^2","title":"Jacobian"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Spectrum","page":"Integrator","text":"The eigenvalues of the 3times3 Jacobian on four test states (Thermodynamics.jl 1.3) show one fast mode near -1tau and two slow modes. With both phases active, one slow mode is slightly positive.\n\nstate phases eigenvalues [s⁻¹]\nwarm updraft, 285 K, tau_l = 5 s liquid −0.200, −2.2e−5, 0\nmixed phase, 261 K, tau_l = 8, tau_i = 60 s liquid + ice −0.141, −3.4e−5, +3.3e−5\nabove freezing, 275 K, tau_l = 10, tau_i = 20 s liquid + ice −0.150, −1.7e−4, +2.8e−5\nice only, 225 K, tau_i = 600 s ice −1.7e−3, −1.7e−8, 0\n\nThe zero eigenvalue belongs to the inactive phase. The matrix functions must therefore be accurate for arguments near zero, of both signs, and far into the left half-plane.","title":"Spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Matrix-functions","page":"Integrator","text":"The Jacobian mixes kg kg⁻¹ and K. Its temperature row, with entries of order L(c_pmtau), dominates lVert hJ_nrVert_1, which on the test states exceeds the magnitude of the eigenvalues by a factor of about 10^3. The package first forms B = D^-1(hJ_n2)D, with D a diagonal of powers of 2 that brings the off-diagonal norms of each row and column within a factor 4 of each other (Parlett & Reinsch 1969, Numer. Math. 13, 293–304), and keeps D = I when B does not have the smaller 1-norm. The similarity is exact, and varphi_k(D B D^-1) = Dvarphi_k(B)D^-1. D depends only on the ratios of the entries of J_n, so it is computed once per linearization and serves every step length tried from it; the iteration for each linearization starts from the D of the previous one.\n\nIt then evaluates the Taylor polynomial of varphi_4 at Z = B2^s, with s the smallest integer that gives lVert ZrVert_1 le 4, to the least degree m with lVert ZrVert_1^m4(m+4) le varepsilon, read from a table of thresholds in Float32 and Float64. The polynomial is evaluated in blocks of four powers (Paterson & Stockmeyer 1973, SIAM J. Comput. 2, 60–66). The package then obtains varphi_3 to varphi_0 from varphi_k-1(Z) = Zvarphi_k(Z) + I(k-1), and doubles the argument s times with (Skaflestad & Wright 2009, Appl. Numer. Math. 59, 783–799)\n\nvarphi_k(2Z) = frac12^kleftvarphi_0(Z)varphi_k(Z)\n  + sum_j=1^k fracvarphi_j(Z)(k-j)right\n\nThe functions at hJ_n2 serve the second stage, and one more doubling gives those at hJ_n. For the mixed-phase test state with h = 60 s, balancing lowers the 1-norm of hJ_n from 16times10^4 to 11 and the doublings from 12 to 2.\n\nAgainst 512-bit references, the error of each of varphi_1 to varphi_4, relative to its largest entry, stays below 27(kappa_k + varepsilon), where the componentwise condition number kappa_k is the summed change of varphi_k, relative to its largest entry, under a relative change varepsilon of each entry of the argument. The matrices tested include Jacobians of test states, matrices with a zero row, a complex pair, or a zero eigenvalue, and norms up to 7times10^5.","title":"Matrix functions"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Step-size-control","page":"Integrator","text":"With hat u_n+1 the embedded solution, u_n+1 - hat u_n+1 = hvarphi_4(hJ_n)(12D_n3 - 48D_n2), and the error of a step is\n\nmathrmerr = max_c fraclvert u_n+1c - hat u_n+1crvert\n  mathrmatol_c + mathrmrtolmax(lvert u_ncrvert lvert u_n+1crvert)\n\nwith mathrmatol_c equal to atol_q for q_l and q_i and atol_T for T. A step is accepted when mathrmerr le 1. The next step size is h minleft(5 maxleft(02 09mathrmerr^-14right)right). The first trial step covers the remaining interval. An rtol below 4varepsilon of the problem's floating-point type is an error.\n\nThe state is accumulated with compensated summation: each addition u_n + Delta u is split exactly into a rounded sum and its rounding error (Knuth's TwoSum), and the error is added to the next increment. Temperature increments smaller than half a unit in the last place of T (15times10^-5 K at 280 K in Float32) therefore accumulate.","title":"Step-size control"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Events","page":"Integrator","text":"The event functions follow the active phases: -q_l for active liquid and delta for inactive liquid; -q_i for active ice; delta_i for inactive ice below T_mathrmtr and for active ice above it, -delta_i for inactive ice with mass above it; and the signed distance past T_mathrmtr. An event happens where one of them becomes positive.\n\nAfter an accepted step, the event functions positive at its end have fired. The one whose straight line between its values at the two ends of the step crosses zero first is located first: its event time s^star in (0 h is a zero of s mapsto g_k(Phi_s(u_n)), where Phi_s is the exprb43 step of length s from u_n with the same J_n, v_n, and D. A bracketing root finder narrows the sign change to a width of 16varepsilon(t_n + h), or of 8r_ks(g_k(s) - g_k(0)) when that is larger, and returns its positive end. Here r_k is varepsilon times the size of the quantities g_k is formed from, so the event time is located to the resolution of g_k at its mean rate over the step. An event function that is positive at s^star and already positive at the other end of the bracket crossed first, and is located in turn on the shorter interval. One that is positive only at s^star fires at s^star as well.\n\nWhen the cubic Hermite interpolant of an event function that did not fire rises above zero on 0 s^star, the step is halved. When it stays below zero, the step is taken to s^star, a phase whose exhaustion fired is set to zero mass, and the active phases are re-evaluated.\n\nWhen no event function is positive at the end of a step, the step is halved if the cubic Hermite interpolant of an event function through its values and time derivatives at the two ends rises above zero, so a turning point cannot hide two crossings inside one step.\n\nThe root finder is the keyword root_finder: BrentRootFinder() (Brent–Dekker iteration with a bisection safeguard) by default, or RootSolversRootFinder(method) with a bracketing method of RootSolvers.jl after using RootSolvers. Both stop at the same width. From an exact zero, Brent steps toward the sign change by the tolerance and doubles the step across a run of zeros. RootSolvers.jl needs a negative value at the lower end, so the extension steps off an exact zero in the same way before calling it and narrows its result to the width by bisection.\n\nThe number of steps is bounded by the keyword max_steps. Exceeding it raises an error.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/integrator/#Thresholds","page":"Integrator","text":"The keyword thresholds takes Thresholds and rounds small quantities at the start of every step. Liquid or ice below x_min returns to the vapor, and the temperature falls by the latent heat of the returned mass, Lxc_pm. A phase without mass forms when its supersaturation exceeds δ_min (liquid) or δ_i_min (ice), or lies within that margin, is not negative, and rises; its event function uses the supersaturation rounded to zero within the margin. A phase with mass uses its supersaturation unrounded. The defaults round nothing.","title":"Thresholds"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Active-phases-and-events","page":"Active phases and events","text":"The three schemes share the rule that decides which phases are active and the list of events that end a segment. This page states both and bounds the number of events in a step of MM2015FixedT and MM2015PiecewiseLinear.","title":"Active phases and events"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Events","page":"Active phases and events","text":"Event Condition Ends a segment when Schemes\nliquid saturation delta = 0 q_l = 0 all\nice saturation delta_i = 0, that is delta = -Delta q_i = 0 below T_mathrmtr; q_i  0 above T_mathrmtr all\nliquid exhausted q_l = 0 while liquid evaporates liquid is active all\nice exhausted q_i = 0 while ice sublimates ice is active all\nequilibrium delta = A_ctau a phase is active MM2015PiecewiseLinear\ntriple-point crossing T = T_mathrmtr always MM2015\n\nA saturation crossing matters where the rate of a phase changes sign with no mass to absorb it: a phase with zero mass forms or starts to lose what it gained, and ice with mass above T_mathrmtr stops or resumes sublimating. At an event the crossed quantity is set to its boundary value, and the active phases are re-evaluated from the new state.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Boundary-rule","page":"Active phases and events","text":"A phase with zero mass that sits exactly on its saturation boundary is active when its supersaturation increases with the phase inactive. For the frozen-coefficient dynamics\n\nf(delta) = A_l - lfracdeltatau_l - ialphafracdelta + Deltatau_iGamma_i\n\nthe liquid term vanishes at delta = 0, so f(0) is the same with liquid active or inactive, and liquid forms when f(0)  0. Below T_mathrmtr, ice with zero mass at delta = -Delta forms when f(-Delta)  0. Above T_mathrmtr, ice with mass at delta = -Delta is active when f(-Delta)  0, that is when it is about to sublimate. The rule also applies when a step starts on a boundary, so no segment has zero length. MM2015 applies the same rule with the time derivative of the full parcel model.","title":"Boundary rule"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Continuity-and-monotonicity","page":"Active phases and events","text":"Hold the condensate presence (q_l  0 or not, q_i  0 or not) fixed. Then:\n\nthe liquid term switches only at delta = 0, where it is zero;\nthe ice term switches only at delta = -Delta, where it is zero, including under the above-freezing rule;\neach active term decreases with delta, with slope -1tau_l or -alpha(tau_iGamma_i).\n\nSo f is continuous, piecewise linear, and non-increasing in delta. The equation ddeltadt = f(delta) has a unique solution, and that solution is monotone in time until the condensate presence changes.","title":"Continuity and monotonicity"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Changes-of-direction","page":"Active phases and events","text":"Presence changes in two ways.\n\nA phase forms from zero mass at its own boundary. Its term is zero there, so f does not jump.\nAn active phase is exhausted. Condensate is lost only while it evaporates or sublimates, that is, while its supersaturation is negative. Its term then adds to f, and removing it lowers f.\n\nf therefore never jumps up. Once f  0, delta falls toward the equilibrium of the current dynamics, f stays non-positive along the way, and later jumps lower it further. delta rises for a while, and then, after at most one change of direction, falls.","title":"Changes of direction"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Bound-on-the-number-of-events","page":"Active phases and events","text":"Each of the two saturation boundaries is crossed at most once while delta rises and once while it falls: at most 4 saturation events.\nA phase is exhausted at most once while delta rises: after it re-forms at its boundary, it stays supersaturated for the rest of the rise. It is exhausted at most once while delta falls. That makes at most 4 exhaustion events for the two phases.\n\nA step of MM2015FixedT has at most 8 events, so at most 9 segments.\n\nMM2015PiecewiseLinear moves delta along the same monotone path with linear pieces and stops it at the equilibrium of the current dynamics. After an equilibrium event delta stays constant, so only an exhaustion can end the next segment: at most one equilibrium event more than the exhaustion events, so at most 5. A step of MM2015PiecewiseLinear has at most 13 events. Exceeding either bound is an error.\n\nFor MM2015 the coefficients change along the trajectory, and the argument above gives no bound; the integrator limits the number of steps.","title":"Bound on the number of events"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Thresholds","page":"Active phases and events","text":"The keyword thresholds of each scheme takes Thresholds. At the start of each segment of MM2015FixedT and MM2015PiecewiseLinear, liquid or ice below x_min returns to the vapor, which raises delta and delta_i by its mass, and a supersaturation below δ_min over liquid or δ_i_min over ice in magnitude is set to zero, where the boundary rule decides the phase. Neither is an event. MM2015 applies the thresholds at the start of each integrator step (Integrator). The defaults round nothing.","title":"Thresholds"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Clear-air","page":"Active phases and events","text":"A segment of MM2015FixedT or MM2015PiecewiseLinear that starts without condensate ends the step when both supersaturations stay negative to the end of the step at the rate A_l: no phase can form, and the rates over the remaining time are zero. Above T_mathrmtr only delta counts, because ice does not form there.","title":"Clear air"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/events/#Triple-point","page":"Active phases and events","text":"Delta = 0 at T_mathrmtr, where both saturation boundaries coincide. The parcel counts as below the triple point when T  T_mathrmtr, or when T = T_mathrmtr and the temperature tendency without latent heating, dot T_mathrmext + dot p(rhoc_pm), is negative. MM2015FixedT and MM2015PiecewiseLinear hold T fixed and decide once at the start of the step. MM2015 decides again at every triple-point crossing.","title":"Triple point"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"benchmark/runbenchmarks.jl times tendencies for every scheme, test state, moisture basis, floating-point type, and thermodynamics backend, and writes the times, allocations, segment and event counts, and floors to benchmark/results/<commit>.json:\n\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/runbenchmarks.jl\n\nEvery call is free of allocations, on every combination; the test suite asserts it.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/performance/#Floor","page":"Performance","text":"The floor of a call is the cost of the transcendental functions it cannot avoid, at the costs the benchmark measures on the machine it runs on:\n\nMM2015PiecewiseLinear: two saturation evaluations (liquid and ice) for the Coefficients.\nMM2015FixedT: the same two, plus one expm1 per segment with an active phase, one log1p per saturation event, and one expm1 per exhaustion event.\nMM2015: six saturation evaluations per integrator step, for the states at its start and at its two inner stages.\n\nA host that supplies its own thermodynamics through coefficients and calls tendencies(scheme, k::Coefficients, Δt) skips the two saturation evaluations.","title":"Floor"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/performance/#What-the-cost-scales-with","page":"Performance","text":"MM2015PiecewiseLinear: one division and a few multiplications per segment; a step has at most 14 segments.\nMM2015FixedT: one expm1 per segment, one log1p per saturation event, and one depletion root per exhaustion event. A depletion root is a closed-form Lambert W seed and at most 8 Newton corrections (Depletion times). Steps without exhaustion cost a few segments; each exhaustion adds a root.\nMM2015: per accepted exprb43 step, one linearization, the varphi_k functions of the 3times3 Jacobian, and three parcel states: at the two inner stages and at its end, which becomes the start of the next step. Locating an event takes 2 to 6 further evaluations of the step map on the test states (Integrator). The number of steps falls as the tolerance loosens.","title":"What the cost scales with"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/performance/#Measured-times","page":"Performance","text":"(Image: Time per call against the floor)\n\nTimes per call (filled) and floors (open), one marker shape per scheme, from the benchmark results of the commit the figure was made from.","title":"Measured times"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/performance/#First-call","page":"Performance","text":"The package compiles tendencies and trajectory for every scheme, moisture basis, and floating-point type with the default backend when it is precompiled, and the Thermodynamics.jl extension does the same for its parameter sets, so the first call in a session does not compile.","title":"First call"},{"category":"section","location":"numerics/performance/#Root-finders","page":"Performance","text":"With RootSolversRootFinder(RootSolvers.BrentsMethod), MM2015 is also free of allocations. On the test states, the Brent iteration of RootSolvers.jl takes up to twice the evaluations of the step map of the default BrentRootFinder() to locate an event: 8 against 4 on activation_cooling.","title":"Root finders"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/moisture_bases/#Moisture-bases","page":"Moisture bases","text":"A problem states its moisture in one of two bases, and the returned rates use the same basis.\n\nBasis Type Definition\nspecific humidity SpecificHumidity() q_x = m_x  m\ndry-air mixing ratio DryAirMixingRatio() r_x = m_x  m_d\n\nHere m_x is the mass of water species x, m_d the dry-air mass, and m the total mass of the parcel (dry air, vapor, and condensate). With q_d = m_dm = 1 - q_t,\n\nq_x = q_d r_x qquad q_d = frac11 + r_t\n\nMorrison & Milbrandt (2015) write mixing ratios; the parcel model page writes specific humidity.","title":"Moisture bases"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/moisture_bases/#The-parcel-model-in-each-basis","page":"Moisture bases","text":"The model has the same form in both bases once every quantity is normalized by the same mass:\n\nQuantity Specific humidity Dry-air mixing ratio\nsaturation over liquid q_sl = q_dvarepsilon e_l(p - e_l) r_sl = varepsilon e_l(p - e_l)\nheat capacity c_pm (per unit moist-air mass) c_p^(d) = c_pmq_d (per unit dry-air mass)\ndensity in dot prho rho rho_d = q_drho\ntotal-water dependence of saturation partial_q_t q_sl = -q_slq_d partial_r_t r_sl = 0\n\nBecause partial_T q_sl = q_dpartial_T r_sl, the products that form the psychrometric factors are the same in both bases,\n\nfracLc_pmpartial_T q_s = fracLc_p^(d)partial_T r_s\n\nso Gamma_l, Gamma_i, alpha, and tau are identical in both bases. The supersaturation and the forcing scale with the basis: delta^(q) = q_ddelta^(r).","title":"The parcel model in each basis"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/moisture_bases/#Closed-parcel","page":"Moisture bases","text":"Without vapor forcing, q_t and q_d stay constant, so every rate converts by the constant factor q_d,\n\nS^(q) = q_d S^(r)\n\nand a problem stated in either basis gives the same physical result.","title":"Closed parcel"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/moisture_bases/#Vapor-forcing","page":"Moisture bases","text":"A vapor forcing changes q_t, and with it q_d. The statement \"liquid and ice change only through phase change\" then refers to the basis of the problem:\n\nin the mixing-ratio basis, r_l and r_i are unchanged by the forcing;\nin the specific-humidity basis, q_l and q_i are unchanged by the forcing.\n\nThe two describe parcels that differ by the change of the normalizing mass, a relative difference of order F_vDelta t in the condensate. Each problem is solved in its own basis, and the converted rates of the two agree to that order.\n\nThe vapor forcings of the two bases relate by differentiating r_v = q_vq_d:\n\nfracdr_vdt = frac1q_dfracdq_vdt + fracq_vq_d^2fracdq_tdt","title":"Vapor forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"#MorrisonMilbrandt2015.jl","page":"Home","text":"Condensation, evaporation, deposition, and sublimation of a homogeneous air parcel over a model time step, after Appendix C of Morrison & Milbrandt (2015). Given the parcel's temperature, pressure, humidities, the relaxation times of its liquid and ice, and the external forcing, the package returns the mean phase-change rates of liquid and ice over the step, with the Wegener–Bergeron–Findeisen exchange between them, the exhaustion of either phase, and activation from clear air.\n\nScheme Temperature Work per call Page\nMM2015PiecewiseLinear coefficients frozen at the step start; rates frozen per segment arithmetic per segment, at most 14 segments MM2015PiecewiseLinear\nMM2015FixedT coefficients frozen at the step start; the exact solution of Appendix C one expm1 per segment and a closed-form root per exhaustion, at most 9 segments MM2015FixedT\nMM2015 evolves; the parcel model solved to a tolerance integrator steps, fewer at a looser tolerance MM2015\n\nEvery call is free of allocations. Performance has the measured times and what they scale with.","title":"MorrisonMilbrandt2015.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"julia> using MorrisonMilbrandt2015\n\njulia> problem = MM2015Problem(\n           SpecificHumidity(),\n           DefaultThermodynamicsBackend(),\n           MM2015State(261.0, 8.0e4, 2.07e-3, 2.0e-4, 1.0e-4),  # T [K], p [Pa], q_t, q_l, q_i [kg kg⁻¹]\n           MM2015Timescales(8.0, 60.0),                        # τ_liq, τ_ice [s]\n           MM2015Forcing(-5.0, 0.0, 0.0),                      # dp/dt [Pa s⁻¹], dT/dt [K s⁻¹], dq_v/dt [s⁻¹]\n       );\n\njulia> validate(problem, 300.0)\n\njulia> map(x -> round(x; sigdigits = 3), tendencies(MM2015FixedT(), problem, 300.0))\n(liq = -6.67e-7, ice = 1.2e-6)\n\njulia> map(x -> round(x; sigdigits = 3), tendencies(MM2015(), problem, 300.0))\n(liq = -6.67e-7, ice = 1.21e-6)\n\nThe parcel sits between ice and liquid saturation at 261 K and rises at about 0.5 m s⁻¹. Its liquid evaporates onto the ice and is exhausted after about 76 s, so the mean liquid rate over 300 s is -q_lDelta t. trajectory returns the same step with its segments or integrator steps recorded, and plot_evolution draws it after using CairoMakie.\n\nThe moisture variables follow the basis of the problem: SpecificHumidity (per unit mass of moist air) or DryAirMixingRatio (per unit mass of dry air), and so do the returned rates. The thermodynamics come from DefaultThermodynamicsBackend or, after using Thermodynamics, from a Thermodynamics.Parameters.ThermodynamicsParameters set.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-the-schemes-do","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Change over the step, between ice and liquid saturation)\n\nChange of liquid, ice, and their sum over one step against the step, for a parcel at 261 K between ice and liquid saturation: the liquid evaporates onto the ice and is exhausted after 26 s, after which its change is -q_l. Dots are a reference solution of the parcel model; the lower panel is the difference of each scheme from it. plot_step_change draws this figure. Five regimes, latent heating, and forcing are in the Gallery.\n\n(Image: Error of each scheme against a reference solution)\n\nLeft: relative error of the mean rates of each scheme against a Radau IIA solution of the parcel model, as a function of the step, for the same state. MM2015 stays below 10^-9; MM2015FixedT differs by 10^-4 to 10^-3 and MM2015PiecewiseLinear by up to 0.28. Right: the error of MM2015 falls with its relative tolerance and stays below it. More in Validation.\n\n(Image: Time per call against the floor)\n\nTime per call of each scheme on each test state, and the floor set by the transcendental functions the call must evaluate (Performance).","title":"What the schemes do"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"Morrison, H., and J. A. Milbrandt, 2015: Parameterization of cloud microphysics based on the prediction of bulk ice particle properties. Part I: Scheme description and idealized tests. J. Atmos. Sci., 72, 287–311, doi:10.1175/JAS-D-14-0065.1.\n\nPages = [\"theory/parcel_model.md\", \"theory/appendix_c.md\", \"theory/moisture_bases.md\", \"schemes/fixed_T.md\", \"schemes/piecewise_linear.md\", \"schemes/t_updating.md\", \"numerics/events.md\", \"numerics/integrator.md\", \"depletion.md\", \"numerics/performance.md\", \"gallery.md\", \"validation.md\", \"api.md\", \"internals.md\"]\nDepth = 1","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Gallery","page":"Gallery","text":"The figures are written by docs/generate_assets/generate_assets.jl, which checks each against the package values it shows: MM2015 against the reference solution at every reference step, event counts and times, and end states against the returned rates. Color marks the phase (liquid, ice, and their sum) and line style the scheme throughout.","title":"Gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Regimes-at-the-start-of-a-step","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Ice tendency and liquid saturation)\n\nSign of the ice rate at the start of a step (fill) at 800 hPa with 0.1 g kg⁻¹ of liquid and of ice and no forcing, against temperature and the vapor mixing ratio relative to liquid saturation. Liquid grows above the line r_v = r_sl and evaporates below it. Below the triple point T_mathrmtr, ice grows wherever the air is supersaturated over ice, including the band between the two saturation curves where liquid evaporates onto the ice. Above T_mathrmtr, ice only sublimates, below its saturation curve, which lies above liquid saturation there: between the two curves liquid grows while ice sublimates. The dots are the five states below: 261 K at r_vr_sl = 1.02, 0.95, and 0.85; 276 K at 1.01; and 295 K at 1.05.","title":"Regimes at the start of a step"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Five-regimes","page":"Gallery","text":"Each state has 0.1 g kg⁻¹ of liquid and of ice (no ice at 295 K), relaxation times of 10 s (liquid) and 50 s (ice), and no forcing, at 800 hPa. The regime names the state at the start of the step; within a step the parcel moves between regimes, and the schemes follow it through saturation and exhaustion events.\n\nThe first figure of each pair shows the change of liquid, ice, and their sum over one step, for steps from 1 s to an hour, on a symmetric logarithmic axis that is linear near zero. Dots are the reference solution of the parcel model. The thin dash-dotted lines are the change at the rate of the start of the step, and the thin solid lines the loss of all of a phase, -q. The lower panel is the difference of each scheme from the reference, relative to the larger of the reference liquid and ice changes; a missing point is an exact match. The second figure follows one step: condensate, supersaturation over liquid and over ice, and temperature, with a dot at each event.","title":"Five regimes"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Supersaturated-over-liquid-and-ice","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Change over the step, supersaturated over liquid and ice)\n\n(Image: One step, supersaturated over liquid and ice)\n\nAt 261 K and r_vr_sl = 1.02 both phases grow. The ice draws the vapor below liquid saturation within seconds, the liquid then evaporates onto the ice, and it is exhausted after 57 s. The liquid change over the step therefore rises, turns, and ends at -q_l. MM2015PiecewiseLinear holds each rate until the next event and reaches the turn later.","title":"Supersaturated over liquid and ice"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Between-ice-and-liquid-saturation","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Change over the step, between ice and liquid saturation)\n\n(Image: One step, between ice and liquid saturation)\n\nAt r_vr_sl = 0.95 the liquid evaporates onto the ice from the start and is exhausted after 26 s. The sum changes sign when the ice growth overtakes the loss of liquid. The evaporation cools the parcel by 0.1 K before the deposition warms it.","title":"Between ice and liquid saturation"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Subsaturated-over-liquid-and-ice","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Change over the step, subsaturated over liquid and ice)\n\n(Image: One step, subsaturated over liquid and ice)\n\nAt r_vr_sl = 0.85 both phases shrink at first. The liquid is exhausted after 7 s, and the vapor it releases lifts the parcel above ice saturation: the ice turns from sublimating to growing, in steps longer than 6 s. MM2015PiecewiseLinear holds each rate until the next event and places the turn at 10 s.","title":"Subsaturated over liquid and ice"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Above-the-triple-point,-between-the-saturations","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Change over the step, above the triple point)\n\n(Image: One step, above the triple point)\n\nAt 276 K and r_vr_sl = 1.01 the air is supersaturated over liquid and subsaturated over ice: the liquid grows while the ice sublimates, until the ice is exhausted after 86 s.","title":"Above the triple point, between the saturations"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Warm,-supersaturated-over-liquid","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Change over the step, warm and supersaturated)\n\n(Image: One step, warm and supersaturated)\n\nAt 295 K and r_vr_sl = 1.05 the liquid takes up a quarter of the excess vapor within a minute and warms the parcel by 0.6 K. The warming raises the saturation humidity, which MM2015 follows; the frozen-coefficient schemes hold it at its start, and MM2015FixedT condenses 1.3 % more.","title":"Warm, supersaturated over liquid"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Latent-heating-and-the-events","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Without latent heating and without events)\n\nMM2015FixedT as computed (solid), with Gamma_l = Gamma_i = alpha = 1 (dashed), and the Appendix C solution C5–C7 to the end of the step with the phases active at its start (dotted). The thin dash-double-dotted lines are the supersaturations delta and delta_i at the start. Without latent heating the warm parcel condenses its whole excess vapor delta; with it, the latent heat raises the saturation humidity and the liquid takes up about deltaGamma_l. Without events the liquid evaporates past its own mass and feeds the ice without bound, through the term (q_sl - q_si)(tau_iGamma_i) of C7; the exhaustion event stops the liquid at -q_l.","title":"Latent heating and the events"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Forcing","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: With and without forcing)\n\nThe state between ice and liquid saturation without forcing and with each of three forcings: ascent at 1 m s⁻¹, cooling at 1 mK s⁻¹, and moistening at 2 mg kg⁻¹ s⁻¹. Each forcing keeps the ice growing after the liquid is gone. Under moistening the deposition warms the parcel from 261 K to 273 K within the hour, which shrinks q_sl - q_si from 0.21 g kg⁻¹ to 0.04 g kg⁻¹ at 3300 s; the parcel reaches liquid saturation after 2740 s and liquid forms again. MM2015 and the reference agree. The frozen-coefficient schemes hold q_sl - q_si at its start, and the supersaturation over liquid settles at A_ltau_iGamma_ialpha - (q_sl - q_si) = -011 g kg⁻¹.","title":"Forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#The-psychrometric-factor","page":"Gallery","text":"(Image: Psychrometric factors)\n\nGamma = 1 + (Lc_p)partial q_spartial T over liquid and over ice at saturation and three pressures. Each phase-change rate is divided by its Gamma: the latent heat released or taken up changes the parcel temperature and with it the saturation humidity, which offsets part of the vapor excess. Gamma is close to 1 in cold air, reaches 4.5 to 7.5 over liquid at 300 K, and is larger at lower pressure, where the saturation mixing ratio is larger.","title":"The psychrometric factor"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Parcel-model","page":"Parcel model","text":"Every scheme in this package approximates one problem: the phase changes of a single homogeneous air parcel that holds water vapor, liquid, and ice, over a time step Delta t. This page states that problem. Appendix C derives Morrison & Milbrandt (2015) Appendix C from it, and each scheme page states which approximation that scheme makes.\n\nThe equations on this page use specific humidity. Moisture bases gives the dry-air mixing-ratio form.","title":"Parcel model"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Variables-and-forcing","page":"Parcel model","text":"Symbol Meaning Unit\nT temperature K\np pressure Pa\nq_t q_l q_i total water, liquid, and ice specific humidity kg kg⁻¹\nq_v = q_t - q_l - q_i vapor specific humidity kg kg⁻¹\ntau_l tau_i supersaturation relaxation times of liquid and of ice s\ndot p pressure tendency Pa s⁻¹\ndot T_mathrmext temperature tendency from radiation and mixing K s⁻¹\nF_v vapor tendency from mixing s⁻¹\n\nLiquid combines cloud water and rain, tau_l^-1 = tau_c^-1 + tau_r^-1 (paper C2). The three forcings are the external terms of the paper's C4 and are held constant over the step. A hydrostatic parcel rising at speed w has dot p = -rho g w. Liquid and ice change only through condensation, evaporation, deposition, and sublimation; the vapor forcing changes q_t at the rate F_v.","title":"Variables and forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Saturation","page":"Parcel model","text":"With varepsilon = R_dR_v and saturation vapor pressures e_l(T) over liquid and e_i(T) over ice,\n\nq_sl = (1 - q_t)fracvarepsilon e_lp - e_l qquad\nq_si = (1 - q_t)fracvarepsilon e_ip - e_i\n\nThe supersaturations over liquid and over ice, and their difference, are\n\ndelta = q_v - q_sl qquad delta_i = q_v - q_si qquad\nDelta = q_sl - q_si qquad delta_i = delta + Delta \n\nDelta  0 below the triple-point temperature T_mathrmtr, Delta  0 above it, and Delta = 0 at T_mathrmtr, where e_l = e_i.\n\nA thermodynamics backend supplies e_l, e_i, the latent heats L_v(T) and L_s(T), and the heat capacities. The backend must satisfy the Clausius–Clapeyron relation with its own latent heats and Kirchhoff's relation with its own heat capacities:\n\nfracdln e_ldT = fracL_v(T)R_v T^2 qquad\nfracdln e_idT = fracL_s(T)R_v T^2 qquad\nfracdL_vdT = c_pv - c_l qquad\nfracdL_sdT = c_pv - c_i \n\nThe partial derivatives of the liquid saturation humidity follow; the ice ones are the same with e_i and L_s:\n\npartial_T q_sl = q_slfracpp - e_lfracL_vR_v T^2 qquad\npartial_p q_sl = -fracq_slp - e_l qquad\npartial_q_t q_sl = -fracq_sl1 - q_t","title":"Saturation"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Psychrometric-factors","page":"Parcel model","text":"The paper's C3 defines the psychrometric corrections of liquid and ice, and C2 and C4 contain a cross factor:\n\nGamma_l = 1 + fracL_vc_pmpartial_T q_sl qquad\nGamma_i = 1 + fracL_sc_pmpartial_T q_si qquad\nalpha = 1 + fracL_sc_pmpartial_T q_sl\n\nwith the moist isobaric heat capacity c_pm = c_pd(1 - q_t) + c_pv q_v + c_l q_l + c_i q_i.","title":"Psychrometric factors"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Active-phases","page":"Parcel model","text":"A phase exchanges mass with the vapor when it is active. The indicators l and i are 1 for an active phase and 0 for an inactive one.\n\nLiquid is active when q_l  0 or delta  0.\nBelow T_mathrmtr, ice is active when q_i  0 or delta_i  0.\nAbove T_mathrmtr, ice is active only when q_i  0 and delta_i  0: existing ice sublimates, and no deposition occurs. Melting is a separate process.\nA phase with zero mass that sits exactly on its saturation boundary (delta = 0 with q_l = 0, or delta_i = 0 with q_i = 0) is active when its supersaturation increases with the phase inactive. Active phases and events shows that this rule makes the dynamics continuous across the boundary.\nAt T = T_mathrmtr exactly, the parcel counts as below the triple point when the temperature tendency without latent heating, dot T_mathrmext + dot p(rhoc_pm), is negative.","title":"Active phases"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Rates","page":"Parcel model","text":"The instantaneous condensation and deposition rates are the paper's C6 and C7 before the time average:\n\nS_l = lfracdeltatau_lGamma_l qquad\nS_i = ifracdelta_itau_iGamma_i","title":"Rates"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Equations-of-motion","page":"Parcel model","text":"fracdq_ldt = S_l qquad\nfracdq_idt = S_i qquad\nc_pmfracdTdt = fracdot prho + c_pmdot T_mathrmext\n  + L_v(T)S_l + L_s(T)S_i\n\nwith p(t) = p_0 + dot pt, q_t(t) = q_t0 + F_vt, and the ideal-gas density rho = p  (R_m T), R_m = R_d(1 - q_t) + R_v q_v.","title":"Equations of motion"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Energy-equation","page":"Parcel model","text":"For a closed parcel the first law is dh = dprho, with the moist enthalpy\n\nh = c_pm(T - T_0) + q_vL_v0 - q_iL_f0\n\nwhere L_v0 and L_f0 = L_s0 - L_v0 are the latent heats at the reference temperature T_0. Differentiating h at constant q_t and using dq_v = -dq_l - dq_i gives\n\nc_pmdT = fracdprho\n  + leftL_v0 + (c_pv - c_l)(T - T_0)right dq_l\n  + leftL_s0 + (c_pv - c_i)(T - T_0)right dq_i\n  = fracdprho + L_v(T)dq_l + L_s(T)dq_i \n\nso the latent heats in the temperature equation are the temperature-dependent ones that also appear in Gamma_l, Gamma_i, and the saturation curves. External heating adds c_pmdot T_mathrmext. The vapor forcing F_v enters at the parcel temperature; any temperature change that mixing causes is part of dot T_mathrmext, as in the paper's C4.","title":"Energy equation"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/parcel_model/#Result-of-a-step","page":"Parcel model","text":"A scheme returns the mean phase-change rates over the step,\n\nbar S_l = fracq_l(Delta t) - q_l(0)Delta t qquad\nbar S_i = fracq_i(Delta t) - q_i(0)Delta t\n\nA step of length zero returns (0 0).","title":"Result of a step"}]
}
